  "text": "חוסר\nᵭ\n\nכאב\nmoidda\nmdamamdk\n\nيורכ\nimportantsigma\nшибка\nbootest\n美麗\norraine\n(主观\n主观\nk t\nelend\nkelend dara\nбара\nbagot\neure\nocha\nchirih\nra\nkut\ngog\nolther\ntaki\nose\nko\nbhang\nm\n\nbglindamam\nbdda\nole\nhoole\nseo\n\nwho\ngok\nole\nole\nhoole\ne\ne\nheb\nkota\nhtoheb\n\n틀\nён\ntb\nurgical\nbujir\nel\n In the triangle \\( PQR \\) with \\( PR = PQ \\), the bisectors of \\( \\angle P \\) and \\( \\angle R \\) intersect at \\( S \\). The circumcircle of \\( \\triangle PSR \\) has a diameter of 14 cm. We need to find the smallest possible area of \\( \\triangle PQR \\).\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Given Information\n- \\( PR = PQ \\): This means \\( \\triangle PQR \\) is isosceles with \\( \\angle P \\) and \\( \\angle Q \\) equal.\n- The circumcircle of \\( \\triangle PSR \\) has a diameter of 14 cm, so its radius is \\( 7 \\) cm.\n\n### Step 2: Analyze \\( \\triangle PSR \\)\nSince \\( PR = PQ \\), and \\( S \\) is the intersection of the angle bisectors of \\( \\angle P \\) and \\( \\angle R \\), \\( S \\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\( PR \\) and \\( PQ \\). This implies that \\( PSR \\) is isosceles with \\( PS = SR \\).\n\n### Step 3: Relate the Circumradius to the Triangle\nThe circumradius \\( R_{PSR} \\) of \\( \\triangle PSR \\) can be related to the sides of the triangle using the formula:\n\\[ R_{PSR} = \\frac{PS \\cdot SR}{2 \\cdot \\text{Area of } \\triangle PSR} \\]\nGiven \\( R_{PSR} = 7 \\) cm, we can express the area in terms of the sides and angles.\n\n### Step 4: Use Trigonometry to Find Angles\nSince \\( \\triangle PQR \\) is isosceles, the base angles \\( \\angle P \\) and \\( \\angle Q \\) are equal. Let \\( \\angle P = \\angle Q = \\theta \\). Then, \\( \\angle R = 180^\\circ - 2\\theta \\).\n\nThe angle bisector of \\( \\angle R \\) divides \\( \\angle R \\) into two equal angles of \\( \\frac{180^\\circ - 2\\theta}{2} = 90^\\circ - \\theta \\).\n\n### Step 5: Apply the Law of Sines\nIn \\( \\triangle PSR \\), using the Law of Sines:\n\\[ \\frac{PS}{\\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta)} = \\frac{SR}{\\sin \\theta} = 2R_{PSR} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{PS}{\\cos \\theta} = \\frac{SR}{\\sin \\theta} = 14 \\]\n\n### Step 6: Find the Area of \\( \\triangle PQR \\)\nThe area of \\( \\triangle PQR \\) can be expressed using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times PR \\times PQ \\times \\sin \\theta \\]\nSince \\( PR = PQ \\), the area simplifies to:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times PR^2 \\times \\sin 2\\theta \\]\nUsing the double-angle identity \\( \\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\), we get:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = PR^2 \\times \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\]\n\n### Step 7: Minimize the Area\nTo minimize the area, we need to minimize \\( \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\). The minimum value of \\( \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\) is \\( 0 \\) when \\( \\theta = 0 \\) or \\( \\theta = 90^\\circ \\), but these cases are not feasible for a triangle.\n\nHowever, as \\( \\theta \\) approaches \\( 0 \\) or \\( 90^\\circ \\), the area approaches \\( 0 \\), which is not practical for a triangle. Therefore, we need to find the smallest non-zero area.\n\n### Step 8: Calculate the Smallest Possible Area\nGiven the constraints, the smallest possible area of \\( \\triangle PQR \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{196 \\text{ cm}^2} \\]",
